answer:
A function is a named piece of code you can run later by calling its name. It can take inputs, called parameters, and give a value back with return.

code:
function add(a, b) {
  return a + b;
}
console.log(add(2, 3));
console.log(typeof add);

real example:
In a Playwright suite you keep helper functions like login(page, user) in one helpers file and call them from every spec, so the login steps are never copy-pasted.

Declaring gives the function a name and puts it in memory. Calling runs the body right now and hands back whatever it returns.

code:
function buildUrl(base, path) {
  return base + path;
}
console.log(buildUrl('https://shop.test', '/cart'));
const saved = buildUrl;
console.log(saved('https://shop.test', '/cart'));

real example:
A spec file declares the test callbacks once at the top and calls them by name inside each test when Playwright decides to run that test.

return hands a value back to the caller and stops the function immediately. Nothing after that return line runs, unless the function is called again.

code:
function firstEven(nums) {
  for (const n of nums) {
    if (n % 2 === 0) return n;
  }
  return undefined;
}
console.log(firstEven([1, 3, 4, 6]));
console.log(firstEven([1, 3, 5]));

real example:
A helper that finds the first failing locator can return it and stop, so the test does not spend time querying elements that are already gone.

The function still runs, but it hands back undefined when it finishes. That is the single most common reason a test helper looks like it does nothing.

code:
function clean(value) {
  value.trim();
}
console.log(clean('  hello  '));
const result = clean('  hello  ');
console.log(result === undefined);

real example:
A fixture helper that trims a token but forgets return gives you undefined, and the login step then sends the literal string undefined as the password.

console.log only writes to the terminal, it changes nothing you can use later. return gives the value to whoever called the function, so it can be stored, compared or chained.

code:
function shout(text) {
  return text.toUpperCase();
}
const stored = shout('hello');
console.log(typeof stored, stored);
console.log(stored.length);
shout('printed nothing above');

real example:
assertion helpers should return the value so tests can chain them, while a debug-only log of the response body is fine to leave as console.log.

Parameters are the names in the function's own list, like width and height. Arguments are the actual values you hand in at the call site.

code:
function greet(greeting, name) {
  return greeting + ', ' + name + '!';
}
const args = ['Hello', 'Ana'];
console.log(greet(...args));
console.log(greet('Hey', 'Bo'));

real example:
A reusable login helper takes user and pass as parameters so every spec can pass its own fixture credentials as arguments.

JavaScript reads your whole script before running any of it, so function declarations are put into memory at the top of their scope. That is why you can call them above the line where they are written.

code:
console.log(sayHi());
function sayHi() {
  return 'hi';
}
console.log(add(1, 2));
function add(a, b) {
  return a + b;
}

real example:
A helpers file full of function declarations can be imported and used from the top of a spec without worrying about file order.

A declaration starts the line with the function keyword and a name, and it hoists so you can call it anywhere in that scope. An expression assigns a function to a variable, so the variable is the thing that gets hoisted, and calling it early throws.

code:
console.log(declared());
function declared() {
  return 'I am hoisted';
}
const expressed = function () {
  return 'I am not hoisted';
};
console.log(expressed());

real example:
In a Playwright fixture file you can declare helper functions with function keyword and call them from a fixture defined above them.

Only the declaration is hoisted, not the assignment. The const binding exists but sits in the temporal dead zone until that exact line runs, so calling it earlier throws Cannot access before initialization.

code:
try {
  notReady();
} catch (err) {
  console.log(err.constructor.name);
  console.log(err.message);
}
const notReady = function () {
  return 'never reached';
};
function ready() {
  return 'fine';
}
console.log(ready());

real example:
Exporting your auth helper as const login = async () => {} and importing it into a file that calls it during module load is the classic version of this error.

It is a function expression that carries a name usable only inside its own body. That name is what lets the function call itself for recursion, and it shows up in stack traces.

code:
const factorial = function fact(n) {
  if (n <= 1) return 1;
  return n * fact(n - 1);
};
console.log(factorial(5));
const anon = function (n) {
  return n * 2;
};
console.log(anon.name, factorial.name);

real example:
A recursive helper that walks a nested response body reads better and debugs better with a named function expression than with an anonymous one.

It is a function expression with no name at all. You write it when the name would add nothing, which is usually when it is passed straight into something as a callback.

code:
const nums = [1, 2, 3];
console.log(nums.map(function (n) {
  return n * 10;
}));
const named = function (n) {
  return n + 1;
};
console.log(named.name);

real example:
Most inline callbacks in an array map or filter in a test file are anonymous, because you only use them for that one call.

An IIFE is an immediately invoked function expression: you write the function and call it on the same line, so it runs once and then disappears. Before modules, it was the way to get a private scope, because every file shared one global scope.

code:
(function () {
  var secret = 'hidden';
  console.log(typeof secret);
})();
const runNow = () => {
  console.log('I ran immediately');
};
runNow();

real example:
Old Cypress files wrapped the whole spec in an IIFE so the helper functions inside did not leak into the next spec file loaded on the same page.

They turn the function into an expression, which is what lets you put a call right after it. Without them the parser expects a function declaration, sees a missing name, and throws a SyntaxError before any code runs.

code:
console.log(!function () {
  console.log('the ! makes it an expression, so it runs');
}());
(function () {
  console.log('or wrap it in parentheses');
})();
console.log(typeof function () {});

real example:
When you paste a snippet into the browser console you often see the ! or the parens, because the console evaluates each line on its own as an expression.

The last declaration wins, because the hoisting step copies them in order and the later copy overwrites the earlier one. Inside another function, an inner declaration shadows the outer name completely.

code:
function role() {
  return 'first';
}
function role() {
  return 'second';
}
function show() {
  function role() {
    return 'inner wins';
  }
  return role();
}
console.log(role());
console.log(show());

real example:
Two specs that both declare a function named login in the same global file will silently use the second one, which is why helpers live in their own module.

In strict mode it stays inside the block. In sloppy mode, which is the default for plain scripts, the browser still hoists it out to the function scope for old code that relied on it, so it can leak.

code:
if (true) {
  function blocky() {
    return 'declared in a block';
  }
}
console.log(typeof blocky);
console.log(blocky());
console.log('this file is sloppy mode; in a module blocky would not exist');

real example:
A helper declared inside an if block inside a Cypress spec can leak to the next spec on the same page, and then two tests disagree about what the helper does.

Functions are values, not just syntax. You can put them in arrays and objects, pass them around, and copy the reference, the same as you would with a string or a number.

code:
const utils = {
  double: (n) => n * 2,
  shout: function (s) {
    return s + '!';
  },
};
const handlers = [utils.double, utils.shout];
console.log(handlers[0](4));
console.log(handlers[1]('go'));
console.log(typeof handlers[0] === 'function');

real example:
A Playwright page object stores its actions as methods on an object, and a test can pick one out and call it later, which only works because functions are values.

Take the original as a value, then call it from inside your wrapper. Copying it with a second name makes a second reference to the same function, it does not clone the behaviour.

code:
function original(n) {
  return n + 1;
}
const wrapped = function (n) {
  return original(n) * 10;
};
console.log(original(1), wrapped(1));
const alias = original;
console.log(alias === original);

real example:
Wrapping page.goto to add a screenshot on failure keeps the original navigation behaviour intact while adding your own reporting step.

Yes. A function declaration behaves like a variable, so assigning to it swaps the function that later calls will use. Code that captured the old reference keeps the old one.

code:
function greet() {
  return 'first version';
}
const before = greet;
greet = function () {
  return 'patched version';
};
console.log(before());
console.log(greet());
console.log(before === greet);

real example:
Test frameworks patch page methods by reassigning them, which is why an arrow or a bound copy taken earlier will not see the patch.

A pure function gives the same output for the same inputs every time and changes nothing outside itself. Tests like that need no cleanup, can run in any order, and can be trusted in parallel.

code:
function tax(subtotal, rate) {
  return Math.round(subtotal * rate * 100) / 100;
}
console.log(tax(100, 0.2));
console.log(tax(100, 0.2) === tax(100, 0.2));
let total = 0;
function impure(n) {
  total = total + n;
  return total;
}
console.log(impure(5), impure(5));

real example:
A pure statusToLabel mapping makes an expect(...).toBe(...) assertion deterministic, while a helper that writes to a module-level array will flake when tests run in parallel.

A side effect is any change your code makes outside of itself: writing to a file, changing a shared object, logging, or sending a request. Pure functions avoid them, most real code cannot.

code:
const sent = [];
function sendReport(name) {
  sent.push(name);
  return sent.length;
}
sendReport('checkout');
sendReport('login');
console.log(sent);
console.log(typeof console.log, Math.random() >= 0);

real example:
A beforeEach hook that deletes files from the reports folder is a side effect, which is why tests using it must not run at the same time.

Returning a new object leaves the original exactly as it was, so two callers holding the same object cannot fight over it. Mutation makes the result depend on the order tests happened to run in.

code:
function addTag(user, tag) {
  return Object.assign({}, user, { tags: user.tags.concat(tag) });
}
const ana = { name: 'Ana', tags: ['admin'] };
const updated = addTag(ana, 'billing');
console.log(ana.tags);
console.log(updated.tags);
console.log(ana === updated);

real example:
Build your Playwright storageState by copying the object instead of pushing into it, or the second spec that logs in will inherit the first spec's tokens.

Nothing about the code changes. A method is a function stored on an object, and when you call it as obj.name() it gets that object as this. Pull it off the object and this is lost.

code:
const cart = {
  total: 10,
  add(n) {
    return this.total + n;
  },
};
const loose = cart.add;
console.log(cart.add(5));
console.log(loose(5));

real example:
Passing page.waitForLoadState as a bare callback loses its this, which is why the Playwright wrappers take a function that receives the page instead.

The engine reads the name off the property key it was assigned to, or off the function keyword name for a declaration. Two functions stored under the same key get the same inferred name.

code:
const obj = {
  greet: function () {
    return 'hi ' + this.who;
  },
  who: 'cart',
};
console.log(obj.greet.name);
function declaration() {}
const inferred = obj.greet;
console.log(declaration.name, inferred.name);

real example:
Naming your helpers meaningfully shows up in the stack trace Playwright prints when an expect fails, which saves a lot of guesswork.

Hoisting only copies function declarations up front. A const arrow function is an assignment, and const sits in the temporal dead zone until that line runs, so calling it earlier throws a ReferenceError.

code:
console.log(early());
function early() {
  return 'declaration wins';
}
try {
  late();
} catch (err) {
  console.log(err.constructor.name);
}
const late = () => 'arrow';

real example:
Defining a page object with const methods at the bottom of the file and using it in a fixture defined at the top gives you this exact error.

It is the stretch between the top of a scope and the line where a let or const is actually assigned. Reading the name in that stretch is a ReferenceError, unlike var which just gives undefined.

code:
try {
  console.log(hoisted);
} catch (err) {
  console.log(err.constructor.name);
}
var hoisted = 1;
try {
  console.log(dead);
} catch (err) {
  console.log(err.constructor.name);
}
let dead = 2;

real example:
A fixtures map built with a const at the bottom of a file and destructured at the top fails with a ReferenceError, not a friendly undefined.

Each call of the outer function creates a brand new inner function, so the two copies are different objects that remember their own parameters. The inner name does not survive the outer call, so nothing outside can reach it.

code:
function outer(tag) {
  function inner() {
    return tag + '!';
  }
  return inner;
}
const a = outer('a');
const b = outer('b');
console.log(a(), b());
console.log(a === b);
console.log(typeof inner);

real example:
A one-off snippet pasted into the Cypress browser console fails to parse if you drop the parens, because the console wraps each line as an expression.

The variable is hoisted, so the name exists, but it holds undefined until the assignment runs. The name is there and still unusable, which is why const or let is the better choice.

code:
console.log(typeof maybe);
var maybe = function () {
  return 'assigned later';
};
console.log(typeof maybe);
maybe();
console.log(maybe());

real example:
A shared options object built with var in one file and read at the top of another gives you undefined and a confusing downstream failure.

You often cannot. Both start with the word function, so the deciding factor is what comes before it: at the start of a statement it is a declaration, after an = or a comma it is an expression.

code:
function declared() {}
const expressed = function () {};
const alsoExpression = function named() {};
const arrow = () => {};
console.log(typeof declared, typeof expressed, typeof alsoExpression, typeof arrow);
console.log(declared.name, expressed.name, alsoExpression.name, arrow.name);

real example:
When you grep a helper file looking for where a function is defined, the keyword alone will not tell you if it can be called before that line.

Nothing observable. Both hand back undefined and both keep running until the end, so the difference only matters when you read the source. Prefer returning nothing explicitly so the intent is clear.

code:
function returnsUndefined() {
  return;
}
function returnsNothing() {}
console.log(returnsUndefined(), returnsNothing());
console.log(returnsUndefined() === returnsNothing());
console.log(typeof returnsNothing());

real example:
A hook helper that returns undefined either way, so reading its output will not tell you whether the teardown step actually ran.

length is defined as the number of parameters before the first one that has a default or a rest, because that is how many arguments the caller must supply. It is a hint about arity, not a count of the parameters.

code:
function plain(a, b, c) {}
function withDefault(a, b = 2, c) {}
function withRest(a, ...rest) {}
function withDestructured({ a }, b) {}
console.log(plain.length, withDefault.length, withRest.length);
console.log(withDestructured.length);

real example:
A small validation helper checks fn.length to warn about a route handler that forgot its second parameter, so the count only makes sense with no defaults.

new creates an empty object, makes it this inside the function, runs the body, and gives you that object. If the body returns a plain object, that object wins instead; if it returns a primitive, the created object still wins.

code:
function Box() {
  this.tag = 'box';
  return { tag: 'plain object wins' };
}
function Silent() {
  this.tag = 'silent';
}
function Plain() {
  return 42;
}
console.log(new Box().tag);
console.log(new Silent().tag);
console.log(Plain(), new (function () { return 42; })());

real example:
Constructing a request helper with new gives every test its own config object, while calling it without new silently returns undefined and breaks the setup.

The parameter wins inside that function, because it is the nearest binding. The same-named function outside is still there, but the body can no longer reach it.

code:
function total(items, sum) {
  return items.reduce((acc, n) => acc + n, sum);
}
console.log(total([1, 2, 3], 10));
function sum() {
  return 'never reached';
}
console.log(typeof sum);
console.log(total([1, 2], 0));

real example:
A fixture that takes a callback parameter called fixture or run silently shadows the helper of the same name, and the fixture body then calls the parameter instead.

throw jumps straight out of the function and unwinds the call stack until something catches it, so the happy path code after it never runs. Returning an error keeps control in the function and forces every caller to check the value.

code:
function parsePort(raw) {
  const n = Number(raw);
  if (!Number.isInteger(n)) throw new TypeError('bad port');
  return n;
}
function tryParse(raw) {
  try {
    return { ok: true, value: parsePort(raw) };
  } catch (err) {
    return { ok: false, value: err.message };
  }
}
console.log(parsePort('8080'));
console.log(tryParse('abc'));

real example:
A request helper that throws on a non-2xx response needs an expect around it, while one that returns { ok, value } can be asserted on line by line.

Parentheses are expression syntax. Wrapping a declaration turns it into an expression, so you must assign it to something for it to have a name. An expression is already an expression, so it can sit inside parentheses or a call.

code:
function atTop() {
  return 'declaration';
}
const wrapped = (function () {
  return 'expression, no name of its own';
});
console.log(atTop());
console.log(wrapped());
console.log(typeof (function () {}));

real example:
Default exporting a helper as export default (function () { return {} }) works because it is an expression, while export default function atTop also works but must keep its own name.
